        "description": "<div id=\"link_wrapped_content\">\n<body><section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p>The Steam Deck, harbinger of a portable PC gaming revolution, has been out of stock for three months. Now it\u2019s back\u2026 at almost double the price of the original model. <a href=\"https://store.steampowered.com/steamdeck\">It\u2019ll cost you $789 USD</a> to get the 512GB OLED version, $949 for the 1TB upgrade. The original LCD model, which debuted at $400, <a href=\"https://www.pcworld.com/article/3018799/valve-retires-the-cheapest-steam-deck-leaving-pricier-oled-as-only-option.html\">is resigned to the dustbin of history</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Welcome to PC gaming in 2026, where the K-shaped economy has claimed the last remaining affordable option. The 512GB OLED model now costs $1,129 in Canada, 649 pounds in the UK, 779 euro in Europe, $1,199 in Australia, and 3,279 PLN in Poland. The 1TB OLED version costs 1,349 CAD/919 euro/779 pounds/1,429 dollarydoos/3,879 PLN. Ouch.</",
